{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01N29HC5TmR43znmsnF4Ks3y","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: rel02.1-uc001-tee\n     2→title: \"Copy stdin to stdout and files via tee\"\n     3→\n     4→summary: |\n     5→  The operator pipes data through the Go tee binary to capture a copy in one or more\n     6→  files while passing the stream through to stdout. The differential testing harness\n     7→  runs both the Go binary and the Homebrew reference binary (gtee from coreutils) with\n     8→  the same arguments, stdin, and environment, then compares stdout and output file\n     9→  contents byte-for-byte.\n    10→\n    11→actor: Human Operator\n    12→\n    13→trigger: |\n    14→  The operator has completed the PRD (prd017-tee.yaml) and invokes do-work to\n    15→  produce a passing differential test for cmd/tee.\n    16→\n    17→flow:\n    18→  - id: F1\n    19→    step: |\n    20→      The operator runs \"printf 'hello\\nworld\\n' | ./bin/tee /tmp/out.txt\". tee writes\n    21→      the data to stdout and to /tmp/out.txt simultaneously.\n    22→  - id: F2\n    23→    step: |\n    24→      The operator runs \"printf 'line1\\n' | ./bin/tee /tmp/a.txt /tmp/b.txt\" to verify\n    25→      multi-file output. tee writes identical content to both files and stdout.\n    26→  - id: F3\n    27→    step: |\n    28→      The operator runs \"printf 'new\\n' | ./bin/tee -a /tmp/existing.txt\" to verify\n    29→      append mode. tee appends to the file without truncating existing content.\n    30→  - id: F4\n    31→    step: |\n    32→      The operator runs \"printf 'passthrough\\n' | ./bin/tee\" with no file arguments.\n    33→      tee copies stdin to stdout only (passthrough mode).\n    34→  - id: F5\n    35→    step: |\n    36→      The differential testing harness (pkg/testutils) executes both ./bin/tee and gtee\n    37→      with the same arguments, stdin, and environment.\n    38→  - id: F6\n    39→    step: |\n    40→      The harness compares stdout byte-for-byte, output file contents byte-for-byte,\n    41→      and exit codes. Both binaries must produce identical results.\n    42→\n    43→touchpoints:\n    44→  - T1: \"cmd/tee — stdin copy, file output, append mode, SIGINT suppression (prd017-tee R1, R2, R3)\"\n    45→  - T2: \"pkg/testutils — RunDiffTests, stdout and file comparison\"\n    46→\n    47→success_criteria:\n    48→  - id: S1\n    49→    criterion: cmd/tee compiles and ./bin/tee exists after mage build.\n    50→  - id: S2\n    51→    criterion: |\n    52→      \"printf 'hello\\nworld\\n' | ./bin/tee /tmp/out.txt\" produces \"hello\\nworld\\n\"\n    53→      on stdout and identical content in /tmp/out.txt, matching gtee.\n    54→  - id: S3\n    55→    criterion: |\n    56→      Multi-file mode works: both output files contain identical content to stdout.\n    57→  - id: S4\n    58→    criterion: |\n    59→      Append mode works: \"tee -a\" appends to existing files without truncating.\n    60→  - id: S5\n    61→    criterion: |\n    62→      Passthrough mode works: tee with no file arguments copies stdin to stdout.\n    63→  - id: S6\n    64→    criterion: |\n    65→      Write-error handling works: tee exits 1 when an output file cannot be opened,\n    66→      prints a diagnostic to stderr, and continues writing to other destinations.\n    67→  - id: S7\n    68→    criterion: |\n    69→      The differential test passes for single file, multiple files, append mode,\n    70→      passthrough, and write-error cases.\n    71→\n    72→out_of_scope:\n    73→  - SIGINT suppression (-i) is tested for compilation but interactive signal delivery\n    74→    is not automated in the differential harness.\n    75→  - Output-error modes (--output-error) and -p flag are excluded per the PRD.\n    76→\n    77→test_suite: test-rel02.1\n    78→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"57d4a27a-e6c5-4554-b927-6d245f0b249e","uuid":"f6ab0f50-a9fa-4495-a34c-2399679039a9","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/341/docs/specs/use-cases/rel02.1-uc001-tee.yaml","content":"id: rel02.1-uc001-tee\ntitle: \"Copy stdin to stdout and files via tee\"\n\nsummary: |\n  The operator pipes data through the Go tee binary to capture a copy in one or more\n  files while passing the stream through to stdout. The differential testing harness\n  runs both the Go binary and the Homebrew reference binary (gtee from coreutils) with\n  the same arguments, stdin, and environment, then compares stdout and output file\n  contents byte-for-byte.\n\nactor: Human Operator\n\ntrigger: |\n  The operator has completed the PRD (prd017-tee.yaml) and invokes do-work to\n  produce a passing differential test for cmd/tee.\n\nflow:\n  - id: F1\n    step: |\n      The operator runs \"printf 'hello\\nworld\\n' | ./bin/tee /tmp/out.txt\". tee writes\n      the data to stdout and to /tmp/out.txt simultaneously.\n  - id: F2\n    step: |\n      The operator runs \"printf 'line1\\n' | ./bin/tee /tmp/a.txt /tmp/b.txt\" to verify\n      multi-file output. tee writes identical content to both files and stdout.\n  - id: F3\n    step: |\n      The operator runs \"printf 'new\\n' | ./bin/tee -a /tmp/existing.txt\" to verify\n      append mode. tee appends to the file without truncating existing content.\n  - id: F4\n    step: |\n      The operator runs \"printf 'passthrough\\n' | ./bin/tee\" with no file arguments.\n      tee copies stdin to stdout only (passthrough mode).\n  - id: F5\n    step: |\n      The differential testing harness (pkg/testutils) executes both ./bin/tee and gtee\n      with the same arguments, stdin, and environment.\n  - id: F6\n    step: |\n      The harness compares stdout byte-for-byte, output file contents byte-for-byte,\n      and exit codes. Both binaries must produce identical results.\n\ntouchpoints:\n  - T1: \"cmd/tee — stdin copy, file output, append mode, SIGINT suppression (prd017-tee R1, R2, R3)\"\n  - T2: \"pkg/testutils — RunDiffTests, stdout and file comparison\"\n\nsuccess_criteria:\n  - id: S1\n    criterion: cmd/tee compiles and ./bin/tee exists after mage build.\n  - id: S2\n    criterion: |\n      \"printf 'hello\\nworld\\n' | ./bin/tee /tmp/out.txt\" produces \"hello\\nworld\\n\"\n      on stdout and identical content in /tmp/out.txt, matching gtee.\n  - id: S3\n    criterion: |\n      Multi-file mode works: both output files contain identical content to stdout.\n  - id: S4\n    criterion: |\n      Append mode works: \"tee -a\" appends to existing files without truncating.\n  - id: S5\n    criterion: |\n      Passthrough mode works: tee with no file arguments copies stdin to stdout.\n  - id: S6\n    criterion: |\n      Write-error handling works: tee exits 1 when an output file cannot be opened,\n      prints a diagnostic to stderr, and continues writing to other destinations.\n  - id: S7\n    criterion: |\n      The differential test passes for single file, multiple files, append mode,\n      passthrough, and write-error cases.\n\nout_of_scope:\n  - SIGINT suppression (-i) is tested for compilation but interactive signal delivery\n    is not automated in the differential harness.\n  - Output-error modes (--output-error) and -p flag are excluded per the PRD.\n\ntest_suite: test-rel02.1\n","numLines":78,"startLine":1,"totalLines":78}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01AX1mdbjYuvcQhLi3PbHqqy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019qh37hLjAdQzrGuekoSvhR","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/341/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd017-tee.yaml"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6835,"cache_read_input_tokens":34841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6835},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"57d4a27a-e6c5-4554-b927-6d245f0b249e","uuid":"8c7a3a9f-3b1e-40d0-92ea-b465c90c1245"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019qh37hLjAdQzrGuekoSvhR","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd017-tee\n     2→title: \"cmd/tee: Read Stdin and Write to Stdout and Files\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  Pipelines often need to capture intermediate output to a file while passing it through\n     6→  to the next stage. tee reads stdin and writes the data to both stdout and one or more\n     7→  named files simultaneously. The macOS BSD tee differs from GNU tee in SIGPIPE handling\n     8→  and append semantics on multiple files. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew\n     9→  GNU reference binary (gtee, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag\n    10→  combinations and multi-file behavior, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n    11→\n    12→goals:\n    13→  - G1: Copy stdin to stdout and to one or more named files simultaneously.\n    14→  - G2: Support append mode (-a) that adds to existing files instead of truncating them.\n    15→  - G3: Handle SIGINT suppression (-i) for use in pipelines where the user may interrupt the downstream consumer.\n    16→\n    17→requirements:\n    18→  R1:\n    19→    title: Core copy behavior\n    20→    items:\n    21→      - R1.1: Must read all bytes from stdin and write them to stdout. Simultaneously, must write the same bytes to each named output file in the order they appear on the command line.\n    22→      - R1.2: When no file arguments are given, must copy stdin to stdout only (passthrough mode).\n    23→      - R1.3: Must create output files that do not exist. Must truncate existing output files to zero length before writing, unless -a is given.\n    24→      - R1.4: When \"-\" appears as a file argument, must treat it as an additional reference to stdout. The data is still written to stdout once (not duplicated), matching GNU tee behavior.\n    25→      - R1.5: Must write output in the order received from stdin. Buffering is permitted but must not reorder data.\n    26→\n    27→  R2:\n    28→    title: Append mode (-a) and SIGINT suppression (-i)\n    29→    items:\n    30→      - R2.1: -a or --append must open each output file in append mode (O_APPEND). Existing file content is preserved and new data is appended after it.\n    31→      - R2.2: -i or --ignore-interrupts must cause tee to ignore the SIGINT signal. Without -i, SIGINT terminates tee normally. With -i, tee continues reading and writing until stdin reaches EOF or a write error occurs.\n    32→      - R2.3: -a and -i may be combined. Their effects are independent.\n    33→\n    34→  R3:\n    35→    title: Error handling and exit codes\n    36→    items:\n    37→      - R3.1: Must exit 0 when all output files and stdout are written successfully.\n    38→      - R3.2: Must exit 1 when any output file cannot be opened or written. Must print a diagnostic message to stderr identifying the failed file.\n    39→      - R3.3: When one output file fails, must continue writing to the remaining output files and stdout. A single file failure does not stop output to other destinations.\n    40→      - R3.4: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout (e.g., broken pipe without -p mode). The default GNU tee behavior exits on EPIPE to stdout.\n    41→\n    42→  R4:\n    43→    title: Differential testing\n    44→    items:\n    45→      - R4.1: Differential tests must compare stdout output, output file content, and exit codes between the Go binary and the reference binary via pkg/testutils.\n    46→      - R4.2: \"Tests must cover: single file, multiple files, append mode (-a), passthrough mode (no files), SIGINT suppression (-i with a signal sent during operation), and write-error handling (output to a read-only path).\"\n    47→      - R4.3: Tests must verify that data written to each output file matches stdout byte-for-byte.\n    48→\n    49→non_goals:\n    50→  - cmd/tee does not implement --output-error modes (warn, warn-nopipe, exit, exit-nopipe) or the -p flag. These are advanced error-handling modes that add complexity beyond the scope of rel02.0.\n    51→  - cmd/tee does not implement output buffering tuning; the default Go bufio buffer size is acceptable.\n    52→  - cmd/tee does not support writing to named pipes (FIFOs) with special semantics beyond what the OS provides.\n    53→\n    54→acceptance_criteria:\n    55→  - \"AC1: printf 'hello\\\\nworld\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/tee /tmp/out.txt produces 'hello\\\\nworld\\\\n' on stdout and identical content in /tmp/out.txt, matching gtee.\"\n    56→  - \"AC2: printf 'line1\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/tee /tmp/a.txt /tmp/b.txt writes identical content to both files, matching gtee.\"\n    57→  - \"AC3: printf 'new\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/tee -a /tmp/existing.txt appends to the file without truncating, matching gtee -a.\"\n    58→  - \"AC4: Differential test against gtee passes for single file, multiple files, append mode, passthrough, and write-error cases.\"\n    59→  - \"AC5: cmd/tee compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    60→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"57d4a27a-e6c5-4554-b927-6d245f0b249e","uuid":"78ac7d75-5ffc-45ae-ba4b-9997f43415f2","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/341/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd017-tee.yaml","content":"id: prd017-tee\ntitle: \"cmd/tee: Read Stdin and Write to Stdout and Files\"\n\nproblem: |\n  Pipelines often need to capture intermediate output to a file while passing it through\n  to the next stage. tee reads stdin and writes the data to both stdout and one or more\n  named files simultaneously. The macOS BSD tee differs from GNU tee in SIGPIPE handling\n  and append semantics on multiple files. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew\n  GNU reference binary (gtee, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag\n  combinations and multi-file behavior, verified by differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Copy stdin to stdout and to one or more named files simultaneously.\n  - G2: Support append mode (-a) that adds to existing files instead of truncating them.\n  - G3: Handle SIGINT suppression (-i) for use in pipelines where the user may interrupt the downstream consumer.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Core copy behavior\n    items:\n      - R1.1: Must read all bytes from stdin and write them to stdout. Simultaneously, must write the same bytes to each named output file in the order they appear on the command line.\n      - R1.2: When no file arguments are given, must copy stdin to stdout only (passthrough mode).\n      - R1.3: Must create output files that do not exist. Must truncate existing output files to zero length before writing, unless -a is given.\n      - R1.4: When \"-\" appears as a file argument, must treat it as an additional reference to stdout. The data is still written to stdout once (not duplicated), matching GNU tee behavior.\n      - R1.5: Must write output in the order received from stdin. Buffering is permitted but must not reorder data.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Append mode (-a) and SIGINT suppression (-i)\n    items:\n      - R2.1: -a or --append must open each output file in append mode (O_APPEND). Existing file content is preserved and new data is appended after it.\n      - R2.2: -i or --ignore-interrupts must cause tee to ignore the SIGINT signal. Without -i, SIGINT terminates tee normally. With -i, tee continues reading and writing until stdin reaches EOF or a write error occurs.\n      - R2.3: -a and -i may be combined. Their effects are independent.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Error handling and exit codes\n    items:\n      - R3.1: Must exit 0 when all output files and stdout are written successfully.\n      - R3.2: Must exit 1 when any output file cannot be opened or written. Must print a diagnostic message to stderr identifying the failed file.\n      - R3.3: When one output file fails, must continue writing to the remaining output files and stdout. A single file failure does not stop output to other destinations.\n      - R3.4: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout (e.g., broken pipe without -p mode). The default GNU tee behavior exits on EPIPE to stdout.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Differential testing\n    items:\n      - R4.1: Differential tests must compare stdout output, output file content, and exit codes between the Go binary and the reference binary via pkg/testutils.\n      - R4.2: \"Tests must cover: single file, multiple files, append mode (-a), passthrough mode (no files), SIGINT suppression (-i with a signal sent during operation), and write-error handling (output to a read-only path).\"\n      - R4.3: Tests must verify that data written to each output file matches stdout byte-for-byte.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - cmd/tee does not implement --output-error modes (warn, warn-nopipe, exit, exit-nopipe) or the -p flag. These are advanced error-handling modes that add complexity beyond the scope of rel02.0.\n  - cmd/tee does not implement output buffering tuning; the default Go bufio buffer size is acceptable.\n  - cmd/tee does not support writing to named pipes (FIFOs) with special semantics beyond what the OS provides.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: printf 'hello\\\\nworld\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/tee /tmp/out.txt produces 'hello\\\\nworld\\\\n' on stdout and identical content in /tmp/out.txt, matching gtee.\"\n  - \"AC2: printf 'line1\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/tee /tmp/a.txt /tmp/b.txt writes identical content to both files, matching gtee.\"\n  - \"AC3: printf 'new\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/tee -a /tmp/existing.txt appends to the file without truncating, matching gtee -a.\"\n  - \"AC4: Differential test against gtee passes for single file, multiple files, append mode, passthrough, and write-error cases.\"\n  - \"AC5: cmd/tee compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n","numLines":60,"startLine":1,"totalLines":60}}}
